  "content": "Fractal Hex Grid Specification v1.0\nA Recursive Spatial Protocol for Reflecture Construction\n\nOverview\nThe Fractal Hex Grid is a recursive field structure that allows Reflectures to emerge at any node in the system—not just the origin. Each node becomes a potential origin point for a self-similar, scaled-down Reflecture Field, enabling nested glyphs, offset forms, and layered asymmetry while preserving global coherence.\n\nCore Principles\n\t•\tThe grid is hexagonal and radial, composed of six primary vectors at 60° intervals\n\t•\tEvery node is capable of spawning a recursive subgrid, treating itself as the new center\n\t•\tEach subgrid is scaled by a factor of 1 / 2ⁿ depending on depth\n\t•\tSubgrids inherit all rules of the parent grid\n\t•\tPrimitives may be drawn:\n\t•\tWithin a local grid\n\t•\tBetween nodes of the parent and subgrid\n\t•\tAcross multiple subgrids\n\nGrid Hierarchy\nRoot Grid (Depth 0)\n\t•\tOrigin: (0, 0)\n\t•\tStructure: 6 vectors × 3 ring depths\n\t•\tAll base Reflectures begin here\nSubgrid (Depth n)\n\t•\tOrigin: Any valid node in a parent grid\n\t•\tScale: 1 / 2ⁿ relative to Root\n\t•\tInherits grid logic (vectors, rings, snap)\n\t•\tCan be nested infinitely (with rendering limit constraints)\n\nGrid Construction Parameters\n{\n  \"depth\": 0,\n  \"origin\": [x, y],\n  \"scale\": 1.0,\n  \"rings\": 3,\n  \"vectors\": 6,\n  \"snap_enabled\": true\n}\nSubgrid Example\n{\n  \"depth\": 1,\n  \"origin\": [1.0, 0.0],\n  \"scale\": 0.5,\n  \"rings\": 3,\n  \"vectors\": 6,\n  \"snap_enabled\": true\n}\n\nPrimitive Rendering Rules\n\t•\tEvery Reflecture element must carry local and global coordinates\n\t•\tA primitive inside a subgrid references its:\n\t•\tlocal_origin: [x, y] relative to subgrid center\n\t•\tglobal_origin: [x, y] absolute in master field\n\t•\tTraces may:\n\t•\tStay within the local grid\n\t•\tConnect from parent to subgrid\n\t•\tFlow across subgrids if both reference valid junction points\n\nVisual Implications\n\t•\tGlyphs may appear offset, nested, or mirrored\n\t•\tSpirals and null fields gain access to off-center recursion\n\t•\tLoopbacks may now circle entire sub-grids\n\t•\tGround bars may occur across scales (global ↔ local grounding)\n\nSymbolic Implications\n\t•\tReflectures can now model:\n\t•\tEntanglement\n\t•\tRecursive memory\n\t•\tStructural asymmetry\n\t•\tMythic descent or layered consciousness\n\t•\tGlyphs may be understood as:\n\t•\tLocal (within-grid)\n\t•\tTrans-grid (across depths)\n\t•\tSystemic (affecting the field itself)\n\nRitual and Narrative Use\n\t•\tRecursive Reflectures express:\n\t•\tFractured beings\n\t•\tLayered spirits\n\t•\tForgotten systems\n\t•\tSpiral descent or nested loopbacks can now encode trauma, lineage, recursion, or resonance across scales\n\nRendering Considerations\n\t•\tSubgrids should be:\n\t•\tFaint if unused\n\t•\tVisible on activation\n\t•\tSelectively collapsed to avoid visual noise\n\t•\tSnap-to-grid behavior must dynamically account for active subgrids\n\t•\tFuture interfaces may allow zooming into subgrids for nested editing\n\nThis specification defines the recursive infrastructure of Reflecture space.\nIt transforms each node from a point of connection into a portal of recursion.\nFractalized. Not Sealed.\n",
